#. Tag: title
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:9
#, no-c-format
msgid "The VI Input Mode"
msgstr "De VI-Ingaavmetood"

#. Tag: title
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:12
#, no-c-format
msgid "VI Input Mode"
msgstr "VI-Ingaavmetood"

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:14
#, no-c-format
msgid "The goal of the VI mode is not to be a complete replacement for Vim and support all Vim’s features. Its aim is to make the <quote>Vim way</quote> of text editing - and the Vim habits learned - available for programs using the &kate; text editor as their internal editor."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:19
#, no-c-format
msgid "The VI mode aims integrate nicely with the programs and deviate from Vim’s behavior where it makes sense. For example, <userinput><command>:w</command></userinput> will open a save dialog in &kate;’s VI mode."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:23
#, no-c-format
msgid "To enable the VI input mode for all new view, go to <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure &kate;...</guimenuitem> <guimenu>Editing</guimenu><guimenuitem>VI Input Mode</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. On this tab you can set options for the VI Input Mode and define and edit the key mapping in this mode. VI Input Mode can also be toggled with the <guimenuitem>VI Input Mode</guimenuitem> setting in the <guimenu>Edit</guimenu> menu. (The default shortcut key is <keycombo action=\"simul\"><keysym>Meta</keysym>&Ctrl;<keycap>V</keycap></keycombo> - where <keysym>Meta</keysym> usually is the <keysym>Windows</keysym> key)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:34
#, no-c-format
msgid "Incompatibilities with Vim"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:36
#, no-c-format
msgid "There are only a few features of &kate;’s VI mode which are incompatible with Vim (not counting things missing). They are listed below together with the respective reasons."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:41
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kate;: <keycap>U</keycap> and <keycombo action=\"simul\">&Ctrl;<keycap>R</keycap></keycombo> is redo."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:43
#, no-c-format
msgid "Vim: <keycombo action=\"simul\">&Ctrl;<keycap>R</keycap></keycombo> is normal redo, <keycap>U</keycap> is used to undo all latest changes on one line."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:45
#, no-c-format
msgid "The reason for having <keycap>U</keycap> act as redo in &kate;’s VI mode is that the shortcut <keycombo action=\"simul\">&Ctrl;<keycap>R</keycap></keycombo> by default is taken by &kate;’s replace function (search and replace). By default, the VI mode won’t override &kate;’s shortcuts (this can be configured in <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure &kate;...</guimenuitem> <guimenu>Editing</guimenu><guimenuitem>Vi Input Mode</guimenuitem></menuchoice>), therefore a redo-action needs to be available as a <quote>regular</quote> key press, too. Besides, the behavior of the <keycap>U</keycap> command in Vim does not map well to &kate;’s internal undo system, so it would be non-trivial to support anyway."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:56
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kate;: <userinput><command>print</command></userinput> shows the <guilabel>Print</guilabel> dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:57
#, no-c-format
msgid "Vim: <userinput><command>print</command></userinput> prints the lines of the given range like its grandfather ed."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:59
#, no-c-format
msgid "Commands like <userinput><command>:print</command></userinput> are available not only in the VI mode but for users using <quote>regular</quote> &kate;, too - therefore the <userinput><command>:print</command></userinput> command opens the print dialog - following the principle of least surprise instead of mimicking Vim’s behavior."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:66
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kate;: <keycap>Y</keycap> yanks to end of line."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:67
#, no-c-format
msgid "Vim: <keycap>Y</keycap> yanks whole line, just like <keycap>y</keycap><keycap>y</keycap>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid "VI’s behavior for the <keycap>Y</keycap> command is in practice a bug. For both change and delete commands, <keycap>c</keycap><keycap>c</keycap>/ <keycap>d</keycap><keycap>d</keycap> will do its action on the current line and <keycap>C</keycap>/<keycap>D</keycap> will work from the cursor column to the end of the line. However, both <keycap>y</keycap><keycap>y</keycap> and <keycap>Y</keycap> yanks the current line. In &kate;’s VI Mode <keycap>Y</keycap> will yank to the end of the line. This is described as <quote>more logical</quote> in the Vim <ulink url=\"http://vimdoc.sourceforge.net/htmldoc/change.html#Y\">documentation</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:82
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kate;: <keycap>O</keycap> and <keycap>o</keycap> opens [<replaceable>count</replaceable>] new lines and puts you in insert mode."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:84
#, no-c-format
msgid "Vim: <keycap>O</keycap> and <keycap>o</keycap> opens a new line and inserts text [<replaceable>count</replaceable>] times when leaving insert mode."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid "This is mostly done as a consequence of witnessing many people being confused by this behavior on a vim irc channel (#vim on freenode)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:94
#, no-c-format
msgid "Switching Modes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:98
#, no-c-format
msgid "<emphasis>Normal Mode</emphasis> permits you to enter commands to navigate or edit a document, and is the default mode. You can return to it from any other mode by pressing &Esc;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:103
#, no-c-format
msgid "<emphasis>Visual Mode</emphasis> permits you to highlight text in a document. Most Normal Mode commands are also valid in this mode. You can enter it by pressing <keycap>v</keycap> to select characters or <keycap>V</keycap> to select lines."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:108
#, no-c-format
msgid "<emphasis>Insert Mode</emphasis> permits you to edit the document directly. You can enter it by pressing <keycap>i</keycap> or one of several other commands listed below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: vi.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid "<emphasis>Command Mode</emphasis> permits you to run certain commands. Press <keycap>:</keycap>, enter the command, and press &Enter;."
msgstr ""

